WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.860
So, in October 2027, the health coverage for about 1.3 million people in Puerto Rico is

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:16.140
going to hit this massive mathematical wall.

00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:18.140
We're talking about the looming Medicaid cliff.

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:19.640
Yes, the huge issue.

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:20.640
Right.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:25.180
And today, for a deep dive, we are looking at this 40-page public finance briefing from

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:27.140
September 2026.

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:31.060
And it basically puts an exact price tag on what is going to cost the local government

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:33.660
just to prevent a total collapse of that healthcare system.

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:35.220
And that price tag is not small.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:36.740
No, it's really not.

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:40.860
It's $4.849 billion in local funds.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:41.860
So welcome to the deep dive.

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:44.220
We're going to be looking at this highly scrutinized report today.

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:48.580
It's called El costo de la Enserquidumbre, or the cost of uncertainty.

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:49.580
Yeah.

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:51.460
And it is a really sobering piece of financial modeling.

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:52.460
Yeah.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:57.180
And when you have a system that provides healthcare for roughly 40% of an entire population,

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:01.460
and they are staring down this like artificial funding expiration date, you need absolute

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:02.460
precision.

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:03.460
You know?

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:04.500
Exactly.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:08.140
And you know, we all know Puerto Rico's Medicaid operates on a completely different

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:14.180
rule book than the 50 states, but actually seeing the math of how these specific legal

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:17.420
mechanisms interact in this report, it's just staggering.

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:18.540
It really is.

00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:23.100
But before we get into the exact local cost of this cliff, I think we should probably

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:25.100
look at who actually ran these numbers.

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:30.140
Because when you throw around a figure like $4.8 billion, the source really matters.

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:31.260
Okay, let's unpack this.

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:33.380
It matters immensely, yeah.

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:37.260
So the organization behind this report is Espacio Subiertos.

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:40.980
They're a research and transparency watchdog based in San Juan.

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:41.980
Okay.

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:45.060
And the report was authored by Angelica V. Morero Sanchez.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:49.660
She's their deputy research director, and she worked with a whole team of analysts.

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:53.260
And you know, they are strictly looking at the public finance reality here.

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:58.540
Their overarching mission is just transparency and public funds and holding the government

00:01:58.540 --> 00:01:59.540
accountable.

00:01:59.540 --> 00:02:03.820
Like a lobbying firm or a PAC trying to score points in Washington or anything?

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:04.820
No, not at all.

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:07.740
They are essentially auditors of public policy.

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:11.740
And the timing of this release in September 2026 is critical.

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:14.100
Right, because things were really heating up then.

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:15.380
Oh, absolutely.

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:19.700
You had the governor of Puerto Rico traveling to Washington, D.C. for meetings and the

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:26.100
entire local healthcare sector, I mean, hospitals, doctors, insurers, they were all publicly

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:27.100
sounding the alarm.

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:32.020
They were basically saying they simply could not wait until 2027 to figure this out.

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:36.140
It sounds like while the politicians were all just arguing about the temperature of

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:40.980
the room, Espacio Subiertos walked in with a finely calibrated thermometer to just read

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:42.420
the actual numbers.

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:43.420
That's a perfect way to put it.

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:46.060
September 2026 was a total boiling point.

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:48.140
The rhetoric was incredibly heated.

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:52.260
So Espacio Subiertos released this brief to just inject cold hard math into the

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:53.260
debate.

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:54.260
Just the facts.

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:55.260
Right.

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:56.260
They didn't want to rely on emotional advocacy.

00:02:56.300 --> 00:03:01.260
They definitively model the local financial burden on Puerto Rico if Congress simply does

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:02.260
nothing.

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:03.260
Okay.

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:07.020
So to understand the temperature they actually recorded, we really need to briefly map out

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:09.820
the mechanics of that bizarre rulebook we mentioned earlier.

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:10.820
We do, yeah.

00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:13.940
Because it adds as the legal structure itself that's creating this cliff.

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:19.540
So in the 50 states and D.C., Medicaid is funded through this open-ended matching

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:20.540
share, right?

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:21.540
The FMAP.

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:22.540
Right.

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:23.540
Federal Medical Assistance Percentage.

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:24.540
Exactly.

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:27.260
It's based on a state's per capita income.

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:32.120
So if a state spends a dollar on a qualifying Medicaid service, the federal government covers

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:33.780
a set percentage.

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:36.380
Usually it's between what, 50 and 83 percent?

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:37.380
Yep.

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:38.380
That's the standard range.

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:42.540
And crucially, there is absolutely no annual dollar ceiling on that.

00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:46.540
And that lack of a ceiling is the defining feature of state Medicaid.

00:03:46.540 --> 00:03:48.020
Let me think about it.

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:52.740
If a state experiences an economic downturn or say a natural disaster or a public

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:57.500
health emergency, and suddenly more people become eligible for Medicaid.

00:03:57.500 --> 00:04:00.660
The federal match just scales up automatically to meet the needs.

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:01.660
Exactly.

00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:02.660
It just adjusts.

00:04:02.660 --> 00:04:05.820
So it's kind of like a discount coupon that never expires and has no maximum limit.

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:08.980
The federal government just keeps paying their percentage no matter how high the total

00:04:08.980 --> 00:04:10.140
bill gets.

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:11.140
That's a great analogy.

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:16.220
But Puerto Rico has to navigate two overlapping restrictions that fundamentally alter how

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:17.460
they can plan their budget.

00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:18.460
Right.

00:04:18.460 --> 00:04:19.460
It's totally different.

00:04:19.460 --> 00:04:22.820
They have a fixed temporary FMAP.

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:28.820
So thanks to a 2023 federal law, it is currently set at 76 percent.

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:34.380
But, and this is key, that elevated match rate is legally mandated to expire on September

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:35.380
30, 2027.

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:36.380
Ah, okay.

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:38.620
So the coupon has a hard expiration date?

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:39.620
Yes, it does.

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:41.700
And the second restriction is far more severe.

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:42.700
Yeah.

00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:46.780
Puerto Rico is subject to an absolute annual dollar ceiling, which is known as the section

00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:47.780
1108 allotment.

00:04:47.780 --> 00:04:48.780
The cap.

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:49.780
Exactly.

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:51.220
It is a strict federal cap.

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:55.940
So once Puerto Rico's Medicaid program hits that ceiling, the federal matching just stops

00:04:55.940 --> 00:04:56.940
entirely.

00:04:56.940 --> 00:04:57.940
Wow.

00:04:57.940 --> 00:04:58.940
Yeah.

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:02.340
From that exact moment forward, Puerto Rico has to pay 100 percent of any remaining

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:05.500
Medicaid costs out of its own local general fund.

00:05:05.500 --> 00:05:08.900
So it's like an expiring coupon layered on top of a gift card.

00:05:08.900 --> 00:05:12.660
And once that gift card is empty, the coupon is utterly useless no matter what percentage

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:13.820
it says on it.

00:05:13.820 --> 00:05:14.820
You're just paying full price.

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:15.820
Exactly.

00:05:15.820 --> 00:05:16.820
You are on your own.

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:19.940
You know, this isn't just some theoretical vulnerability either.

00:05:19.940 --> 00:05:23.980
I really want to bring up the historical context that was included in the sources because it

00:05:23.980 --> 00:05:26.580
proves how rigid this legal machine actually is.

00:05:26.580 --> 00:05:30.940
Like we saw a massive warning shot regarding the fiscal year 2022 budget.

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:31.940
Oh, yeah.

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:36.100
What's fascinating here is that that was a defining moment for public finance in Puerto

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:37.100
Rico.

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:40.900
Because right after the initial hurricane and COVID federal supplements ended, Puerto

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:43.140
Rico faced a very similar reset, right?

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:44.140
Right.

00:05:44.140 --> 00:05:48.140
The recovery formula was going to force Puerto Rico's funding cap back to a tiny base year

00:05:48.140 --> 00:05:49.820
from 2019.

00:05:49.820 --> 00:05:53.260
It would have dropped the annual cap to just over $400 million.

00:05:53.260 --> 00:05:55.580
Which is nothing for a program that size.

00:05:55.580 --> 00:05:56.580
Right.

00:05:56.580 --> 00:05:59.700
So the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services, CMS, they looked at this and

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:01.860
essentially declared it an absurd result.

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:03.500
They saw it was unworkable.

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:04.500
Completely.

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:05.500
Right.

00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:07.060
So CMS tried to implement a workaround.

00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:11.860
They bypassed 2019, used a 2020 base year instead, and attempted to assign Puerto

00:06:11.860 --> 00:06:18.100
Rico $2.943 billion to avert an immediate health care crisis.

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:22.780
So on the surface, that just looks like the federal agency recognizing a mathematical

00:06:22.780 --> 00:06:25.740
glitch and fixing it administratively, you know?

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:26.740
It does.

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:30.260
But the government accountability office, the GAO, they stepped in and completely

00:06:30.260 --> 00:06:31.260
struck it down.

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:32.260
Wait, really?

00:06:32.260 --> 00:06:33.260
Yeah.

00:06:33.260 --> 00:06:36.500
They ruled that CMS's administrative workaround was legally unauthorized.

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:37.500
Wow.

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:40.740
So CMS tried to help and the GAO said, nope, you can't do that.

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:41.740
Exactly.

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:45.580
The Congress then had to scramble and pass a whole new law to temporarily set the caps

00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:47.540
we have today.

00:06:47.540 --> 00:06:52.020
But, and this is the absolute crux of the crisis for 2027, when Congress wrote that

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:55.020
new law, they deliberately closed that loophole.

00:06:55.020 --> 00:06:56.020
Oh, wow.

00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:57.020
Yeah.

00:06:57.020 --> 00:07:00.180
They explicitly wrote the statute so that CMS can never use an alternative base

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:01.500
year again.

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:07.220
The law literally forces the calculation back to that catastrophic 2019 base year

00:07:07.220 --> 00:07:08.700
for fiscal year 2028.

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:10.740
So they basically locked the door and threw away the key.

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:11.740
They did.

00:07:11.740 --> 00:07:15.780
And that rigid legal reality is what Espacio Sabierto has fed into their financial

00:07:15.780 --> 00:07:18.740
models, which leads us to scenario one in their report.

00:07:18.740 --> 00:07:19.740
The worst case scenario.

00:07:19.740 --> 00:07:20.740
Yes.

00:07:20.740 --> 00:07:21.740
Okay.

00:07:21.740 --> 00:07:23.740
This is what happens mechanically if Congress does absolutely nothing.

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:24.740
Okay.

00:07:24.740 --> 00:07:32.300
So if Congress does nothing on October 1st, 2027, the FMAP drops from 76% to 55%.

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:34.620
But the really devastating blow is the cap.

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:38.780
Because of that forced recalculation back to the 2019 base year, the annual

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:46.540
ceiling plunges from $3.825 billion down to an estimated $471 million.

00:07:46.540 --> 00:07:47.620
It's wild.

00:07:47.620 --> 00:07:53.860
That is essentially a business having its primary line of credit slashed by nearly 90% overnight

00:07:53.860 --> 00:07:56.540
based on some arbitrary algorithm from eight years prior.

00:07:56.540 --> 00:07:59.820
So what did Espacio Sabierto actually calculate based on that?

00:07:59.820 --> 00:08:02.580
They built what they call a hold coverage constant model.

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:04.940
So they asked a very specific question.

00:08:04.940 --> 00:08:08.380
What exact dollar amount would it cost the local government to keep the current

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:13.180
1.3 million enrollees covered with the exact same benefits they have today under these

00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:14.340
new brutal rules?

00:08:14.340 --> 00:08:18.140
Just keeping the lights on, no expansions, no catching up to the state parity, just

00:08:18.140 --> 00:08:19.140
the status quo.

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:20.140
Exactly.

00:08:20.140 --> 00:08:21.860
And the number they arrived at is paralyzing.

00:08:21.860 --> 00:08:25.020
To maintain the current program, the local Puerto Rican government would have to somehow

00:08:25.020 --> 00:08:30.300
find $4.849 billion in local funds for that single fiscal year.

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:31.660
$4.8 billion.

00:08:31.660 --> 00:08:35.340
Just to understand the gravity of that number, you really have to look at the entire

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:36.460
Puerto Rican budget.

00:08:36.460 --> 00:08:44.060
I mean, that $4.849 billion, that would consume 35.4% of Puerto Rico's entire

00:08:44.060 --> 00:08:44.820
general fund.

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:48.860
Over a third of all local tax revenues and resources.

00:08:48.860 --> 00:08:49.180
Yeah.

00:08:49.180 --> 00:08:52.700
That is the equivalent of the budgets for the Department of Education, the police

00:08:52.700 --> 00:08:56.620
force, the university system, the courts, and several other major agencies

00:08:56.620 --> 00:09:00.180
entirely combined just poured into Medicaid to fill a federal hole.

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:03.580
It is mathematically impossible for the local government to absorb.

00:09:03.580 --> 00:09:04.420
It really is.

00:09:04.460 --> 00:09:08.940
And you know, Espacio Zabiertos' modeling reveals something highly counterintuitive

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:11.900
about where that financial hole actually comes from.

00:09:11.900 --> 00:09:15.100
Because we focus a lot on the drop in the matching rate, right?

00:09:15.100 --> 00:09:17.020
From 76 down to 55%.

00:09:17.020 --> 00:09:18.540
Sure, people fix it on the percentage.

00:09:18.540 --> 00:09:22.540
But their analytical finding proves that the FMAP drop isn't actually the

00:09:22.540 --> 00:09:24.260
primary driver of the crisis.

00:09:24.260 --> 00:09:26.300
The cap is the true villain here.

00:09:26.300 --> 00:09:29.300
Right, because if you just run the numbers on the percentage, they

00:09:29.300 --> 00:09:31.300
don't look as catastrophic on their own.

00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:31.940
Exactly.

00:09:31.940 --> 00:09:38.100
So if Puerto Rico simply had a 55% FMAP, like a typical state, but with no cap,

00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:42.780
the federal government would pay $2.926 billion for that year.

00:09:42.780 --> 00:09:43.580
Right.

00:09:43.580 --> 00:09:48.460
But because the cap is legally locked at an estimated $471 million,

00:09:48.460 --> 00:09:52.860
it artificially creates an extra $2.46 billion hole.

00:09:52.860 --> 00:09:55.220
The cap is what creates the cliff.

00:09:55.220 --> 00:09:58.540
Raising the percentage match without lifting the cap just means Puerto Rico

00:09:58.540 --> 00:10:01.060
drains its tiny allotment even faster.

00:10:01.060 --> 00:10:04.260
And this actually brings up a point I really need to clarify.

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:09.060
Because when this report dropped in September 2026, the media response was

00:10:09.060 --> 00:10:10.260
intense.

00:10:10.260 --> 00:10:11.180
Oh, I remember.

00:10:11.180 --> 00:10:14.900
I mean, I saw viral headlines everywhere claiming that this specific

00:10:14.900 --> 00:10:19.540
Espacio-Sabirito's report predicted like 700,000 to a million people

00:10:19.540 --> 00:10:21.420
in Puerto Rico were going to lose their health coverage.

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:22.540
Wait, I saw those headlines too.

00:10:22.540 --> 00:10:23.740
Is that what EA calculated?

00:10:23.740 --> 00:10:24.740
No, not at all.

00:10:24.740 --> 00:10:27.500
When you read the actual methodology in the sources, that isn't

00:10:27.500 --> 00:10:28.940
what their math calculated.

00:10:28.940 --> 00:10:32.300
Precision is absolutely vital when dealing with public finance.

00:10:32.300 --> 00:10:35.100
And Espacio-Sabirito's did not calculate this enrollment.

00:10:35.100 --> 00:10:38.180
OK, so they didn't predict the million people losing coverage?

00:10:38.180 --> 00:10:41.060
No, they didn't build a demographic model,

00:10:41.060 --> 00:10:43.540
predicting exactly who loses coverage

00:10:43.540 --> 00:10:46.020
or how eligibility lines would be redrawn.

00:10:46.020 --> 00:10:48.460
That 700,000 to a million figure

00:10:48.460 --> 00:10:52.100
was actually just a secondary citation in their introduction.

00:10:52.100 --> 00:10:53.060
Oh, I see.

00:10:53.060 --> 00:10:55.060
They were simply quoting a warning

00:10:55.060 --> 00:10:57.220
that the local government and the hospital sector

00:10:57.220 --> 00:11:00.060
had already been publicly repeating for months.

00:11:00.060 --> 00:11:04.300
So the media basically took the local government's panic number

00:11:04.300 --> 00:11:08.020
and attributed it to this very dense, precise financial report

00:11:08.020 --> 00:11:09.180
just to give it more weight?

00:11:09.180 --> 00:11:13.300
Yes, which really obscures the actual value of the report.

00:11:13.300 --> 00:11:15.860
Espacio-Sabirito's model was purely financial.

00:11:15.860 --> 00:11:19.060
It was designed to calculate the exact dollar amount required

00:11:19.060 --> 00:11:20.500
to prevent that disenrollment.

00:11:20.500 --> 00:11:21.140
Got it.

00:11:21.140 --> 00:11:25.900
If the local government cannot instantly materialize $4.8-$4.9 billion,

00:11:25.900 --> 00:11:27.940
then yes, catastrophic disenrollment happens,

00:11:27.940 --> 00:11:31.180
provider rates get slashed, and doctors flee the island.

00:11:31.180 --> 00:11:34.020
But the report itself is just a price tag on the status quo.

00:11:34.020 --> 00:11:36.420
OK, so if scenario one is the cliff,

00:11:36.420 --> 00:11:38.100
what do they model for the alternatives?

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:40.380
Because obviously, nobody in Washington or San Juan

00:11:40.380 --> 00:11:42.060
wants a complete systemic collapse.

00:11:42.060 --> 00:11:44.260
So this leads us to scenario two,

00:11:44.260 --> 00:11:47.340
which looks at what happens if Congress just

00:11:47.340 --> 00:11:49.500
applies a legislative band-aid.

00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:52.780
Right, so scenario two models a simple extension

00:11:52.780 --> 00:11:55.780
of the current rules, but adjusting the federal cap

00:11:55.780 --> 00:11:56.980
for inflation.

00:11:56.980 --> 00:11:59.900
Specifically, they use the standard medical inflation

00:11:59.900 --> 00:12:04.540
rate, the medical CPIU, which sits at roughly 2.6% a year.

00:12:04.540 --> 00:12:05.260
OK.

00:12:05.260 --> 00:12:08.220
In that scenario, the local cost drops dramatically

00:12:08.220 --> 00:12:13.460
from $4.8 billion down to $1.396 billion,

00:12:13.460 --> 00:12:15.380
which is about 10% of the general fund.

00:12:15.380 --> 00:12:18.340
I mean, that seems entirely manageable compared to 35%.

00:12:18.340 --> 00:12:21.060
So why doesn't Congress just index the cap to inflation

00:12:21.060 --> 00:12:22.060
and call it a day?

00:12:22.060 --> 00:12:24.180
Because Espacio Severto's points out

00:12:24.180 --> 00:12:26.340
a fatal flaw in the band-aid approach.

00:12:26.340 --> 00:12:28.380
Standard medical inflation just does not

00:12:28.380 --> 00:12:30.580
reflect the reality on the ground in Puerto Rico.

00:12:30.580 --> 00:12:34.340
Actual Medicaid costs, they're growing at about 5.7% a year.

00:12:34.340 --> 00:12:36.660
Wait, why is there such a massive discrepancy?

00:12:36.660 --> 00:12:38.540
Why is Puerto Rico's health care inflation

00:12:38.540 --> 00:12:41.540
running more than double the standard medical CPI?

00:12:41.540 --> 00:12:43.060
It really comes down to catching up

00:12:43.060 --> 00:12:44.580
on decades of deferred maintenance

00:12:44.580 --> 00:12:46.580
and artificially suppressed provider rates.

00:12:46.580 --> 00:12:48.860
Oh, because they've been underfunded for so long.

00:12:48.860 --> 00:12:49.940
Exactly.

00:12:49.940 --> 00:12:52.260
For years, doctors and hospitals in Puerto Rico

00:12:52.260 --> 00:12:54.500
have been paid significantly less than their mainland

00:12:54.500 --> 00:12:55.940
counterparts.

00:12:55.940 --> 00:12:58.660
To stop the massive exodus of medical professionals

00:12:58.660 --> 00:13:00.620
leaving the island, the local system

00:13:00.620 --> 00:13:02.820
has to rapidly increase reimbursement rates.

00:13:02.820 --> 00:13:03.340
Great.

00:13:03.340 --> 00:13:04.940
You have to pay doctors to keep them there.

00:13:04.940 --> 00:13:06.020
Exactly.

00:13:06.020 --> 00:13:08.820
And furthermore, the population is aging rapidly,

00:13:08.820 --> 00:13:11.300
which requires more complex care.

00:13:11.300 --> 00:13:14.820
So if the federal cap only grows at 2.6%,

00:13:14.820 --> 00:13:18.820
but the actual cost of providing care grows at 5.7%,

00:13:18.820 --> 00:13:21.420
the local government has to cover a wider and wider

00:13:21.420 --> 00:13:23.460
financial gap every single year.

00:13:23.460 --> 00:13:24.980
So the Band-Aid eventually rips.

00:13:24.980 --> 00:13:25.500
Exactly.

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:27.820
It's a slow-motion cliff instead of an overnight one.

00:13:27.820 --> 00:13:29.740
OK, so if the Band-Aid approach fails

00:13:29.740 --> 00:13:31.980
because it ignores real-cost growth,

00:13:31.980 --> 00:13:35.300
Espacio-Sobillartos must have modeled a permanent fix.

00:13:35.300 --> 00:13:38.340
What does actual equality look like in their math?

00:13:38.340 --> 00:13:40.820
That is scenario three, the parody ask.

00:13:40.820 --> 00:13:42.660
They modeled what it would cost to Puerto Rico

00:13:42.660 --> 00:13:44.940
got the exact same deal as the 50 states.

00:13:44.940 --> 00:13:45.540
Which means?

00:13:45.540 --> 00:13:48.780
This means an 83% FMAP, which

00:13:48.980 --> 00:13:51.900
by the way, is the statutory maximum they would qualify for

00:13:51.900 --> 00:13:55.060
based on their local poverty levels and absolutely no cap.

00:13:55.060 --> 00:13:57.700
The permanent coupon with no gift card limit.

00:13:57.700 --> 00:13:58.660
Exactly.

00:13:58.660 --> 00:14:01.140
And in this parody scenario, the local cost

00:14:01.140 --> 00:14:04.260
drops to $905 million.

00:14:04.260 --> 00:14:06.620
That is only 6.6% of the general fund.

00:14:06.620 --> 00:14:08.060
Wow, that's a massive difference.

00:14:08.060 --> 00:14:08.700
It really is.

00:14:08.700 --> 00:14:10.340
It stabilizes the system.

00:14:10.340 --> 00:14:12.180
It makes budgeting predictable.

00:14:12.180 --> 00:14:14.140
And it aligns Puerto Rico's financial burden

00:14:14.140 --> 00:14:16.620
with states of similar economic demographics.

00:14:16.620 --> 00:14:19.540
And here's where it gets really interesting

00:14:19.540 --> 00:14:22.180
because we really need to pause and examine

00:14:22.180 --> 00:14:24.140
the status quo we keep mentioning.

00:14:24.140 --> 00:14:26.500
Because even right now, prior to the cliff

00:14:26.500 --> 00:14:29.620
with the elevated temporary funding they currently receive,

00:14:29.620 --> 00:14:32.300
Puerto Rico's Medicaid program is not

00:14:32.300 --> 00:14:33.980
operating at parity with the state.

00:14:33.980 --> 00:14:35.180
Not even close, no.

00:14:35.180 --> 00:14:37.740
The report details this massive benefit gap

00:14:37.740 --> 00:14:39.780
that is just physically impacting

00:14:39.780 --> 00:14:41.020
the healthcare infrastructure.

00:14:41.020 --> 00:14:42.820
Yeah, this is one of the most illuminating sections

00:14:42.820 --> 00:14:43.860
of the report.

00:14:43.860 --> 00:14:46.340
Because Puerto Rico has operated under these strict

00:14:46.340 --> 00:14:48.860
federal caps for decades, the local government

00:14:48.860 --> 00:14:51.180
has historically had to ration care.

00:14:51.180 --> 00:14:52.780
They simply didn't have the funding

00:14:52.780 --> 00:14:55.620
to offer a full state-like benefit package.

00:14:55.620 --> 00:14:58.300
Yeah, I saw in the sources that certain basic services

00:14:58.300 --> 00:15:01.180
were only just added in July 2024.

00:15:01.180 --> 00:15:02.300
Yes.

00:15:02.300 --> 00:15:04.700
I mean, think about the physical realities of this.

00:15:04.700 --> 00:15:08.460
Until July 2024, Puerto Rico's Medicaid

00:15:08.460 --> 00:15:11.700
did not cover non-emergency medical transportation.

00:15:11.700 --> 00:15:14.260
They did not cover durable medical equipment,

00:15:14.300 --> 00:15:16.540
which means basic necessities like wheelchairs

00:15:16.540 --> 00:15:18.980
or oxygen tanks for home use weren't covered.

00:15:18.980 --> 00:15:20.340
That is deeply tragic.

00:15:20.340 --> 00:15:21.660
They didn't cover home health aids

00:15:21.660 --> 00:15:23.620
and they didn't cover hospice care.

00:15:23.620 --> 00:15:26.140
I mean, those are mandatory foundational services

00:15:26.140 --> 00:15:27.500
in the 50 states.

00:15:27.500 --> 00:15:29.420
If you couldn't get coverage for a wheelchair

00:15:29.420 --> 00:15:33.300
or home health until 2024, how did the system even function?

00:15:33.300 --> 00:15:35.860
Well, it functioned by shifting the burden.

00:15:35.860 --> 00:15:39.140
If a patient can't get home health care or a wheelchair,

00:15:39.140 --> 00:15:41.180
they often just end up staying in a hospital bed

00:15:41.180 --> 00:15:42.900
far longer than medically necessary,

00:15:42.940 --> 00:15:45.340
simply because they cannot be safely discharged.

00:15:45.340 --> 00:15:46.180
Oh, man.

00:15:46.180 --> 00:15:48.260
So that creates a bottleneck in the hospitals.

00:15:48.260 --> 00:15:49.100
Exactly.

00:15:49.100 --> 00:15:51.860
Which in turn spikes the overall cost of care.

00:15:51.860 --> 00:15:53.580
This rationing of basic services

00:15:53.580 --> 00:15:55.660
is exactly why their healthcare inflation

00:15:55.660 --> 00:15:57.740
is running at 5.7%.

00:15:57.740 --> 00:15:59.420
And Espacio-Sobirto has calculated

00:15:59.420 --> 00:16:01.740
that just keeping those four new benefits

00:16:01.740 --> 00:16:03.940
they added in 2024 is gonna cost

00:16:03.940 --> 00:16:06.500
over $500 million in future years.

00:16:06.500 --> 00:16:08.700
Wait, that one-line item,

00:16:08.700 --> 00:16:10.700
just keeping wheelchairs and home health,

00:16:10.700 --> 00:16:14.980
costs more than the entire $471 million federal cap

00:16:14.980 --> 00:16:17.820
they would receive if they fall off the cliff in 2027.

00:16:17.820 --> 00:16:18.660
Precisely.

00:16:18.660 --> 00:16:20.500
And the rationing isn't just about services,

00:16:20.500 --> 00:16:22.340
it's about who actually gets them.

00:16:22.340 --> 00:16:24.140
Historically, Puerto Rico had to set

00:16:24.140 --> 00:16:26.580
its local poverty eligibility line

00:16:26.580 --> 00:16:29.420
significantly lower than the federal poverty line,

00:16:29.420 --> 00:16:30.620
just to limit the number of people

00:16:30.620 --> 00:16:32.700
who could enroll and drain the capped funds.

00:16:32.700 --> 00:16:33.540
Right.

00:16:33.540 --> 00:16:34.820
They finally moved that line up

00:16:34.820 --> 00:16:37.100
closer to federal parity in 2023,

00:16:37.100 --> 00:16:39.540
which costs $29 million a year.

00:16:39.540 --> 00:16:41.780
But even today, a massive piece of the puzzle

00:16:41.780 --> 00:16:42.620
is still missing.

00:16:42.620 --> 00:16:44.380
Long-term nursing facility care.

00:16:44.380 --> 00:16:45.220
Correct.

00:16:45.220 --> 00:16:47.540
Nursing home care is a massive mandatory expense

00:16:47.540 --> 00:16:49.180
in state Medicaid programs,

00:16:49.180 --> 00:16:51.900
but it is still largely absent from the Puerto Rican system.

00:16:51.900 --> 00:16:54.900
The infrastructure simply has been funded to support it.

00:16:54.900 --> 00:16:57.980
Okay, so we have the horrifying math of the cliff.

00:16:57.980 --> 00:17:01.140
We see why a simple inflation adjustment fails.

00:17:01.140 --> 00:17:03.020
And we see the systemic degradation

00:17:03.020 --> 00:17:05.820
caused by decades of rationing care.

00:17:05.820 --> 00:17:08.820
What is Espacio-Sobirto's actual plan?

00:17:08.820 --> 00:17:11.100
Because a transparency organization

00:17:11.100 --> 00:17:13.540
doesn't just publish a 40-page model of a crisis

00:17:13.540 --> 00:17:15.940
without presenting a concrete set of recommendations

00:17:15.940 --> 00:17:16.780
to avoid it.

00:17:16.780 --> 00:17:17.620
No, they don't.

00:17:17.620 --> 00:17:21.140
And their recommendations are fierce, highly specific,

00:17:21.140 --> 00:17:23.300
and entirely driven by the financial mechanics

00:17:23.300 --> 00:17:24.580
we just unpacked.

00:17:24.580 --> 00:17:27.340
They direct their demands at two distinct targets,

00:17:27.340 --> 00:17:28.780
the federal government in Washington

00:17:28.780 --> 00:17:30.980
and the local government in San Juan.

00:17:30.980 --> 00:17:32.540
Okay, let's start with Congress.

00:17:32.540 --> 00:17:33.980
What is the demand there?

00:17:33.980 --> 00:17:35.700
To the United States Congress,

00:17:35.700 --> 00:17:37.700
their first and absolute strongest preference

00:17:37.740 --> 00:17:39.380
is structural parity.

00:17:39.380 --> 00:17:43.140
The total elimination of the Section 1108 allotment cap

00:17:43.140 --> 00:17:46.220
and setting of the FMAP by the standard state formula,

00:17:46.220 --> 00:17:48.700
which would yield that 83% match,

00:17:48.700 --> 00:17:51.100
basically fixed the structural flaw permanently.

00:17:51.100 --> 00:17:53.500
But I mean, they are public finance auditors,

00:17:53.500 --> 00:17:54.740
which means they are realists.

00:17:54.740 --> 00:17:57.100
They know Congress might balk at full parity.

00:17:57.100 --> 00:17:59.140
Right, so they have contingency demand.

00:17:59.140 --> 00:18:01.260
If Congress refuses parity

00:18:01.260 --> 00:18:03.900
and will only pass an extension of the current rules,

00:18:03.900 --> 00:18:06.900
Espacio-Sobirto's demands three specific terms.

00:18:06.900 --> 00:18:07.780
Okay, what are they?

00:18:07.780 --> 00:18:10.820
First, the extension must be a minimum of five years.

00:18:10.820 --> 00:18:12.500
You cannot run a healthcare system

00:18:12.500 --> 00:18:14.500
on one or two year funding patches.

00:18:14.500 --> 00:18:15.340
Right.

00:18:15.340 --> 00:18:17.460
Second, the cap must be indexed

00:18:17.460 --> 00:18:20.820
to the actual Medicaid cost growth in Puerto Rico

00:18:20.820 --> 00:18:25.820
that 5.7%, not the artificially low 2.6% medical CPI.

00:18:25.980 --> 00:18:27.260
Otherwise, you're just guaranteeing

00:18:27.260 --> 00:18:29.020
a local deficit every single year.

00:18:29.020 --> 00:18:29.860
Exactly.

00:18:29.860 --> 00:18:32.820
And third, Congress most permanently rewrite the statute

00:18:32.820 --> 00:18:34.780
so it never ever snaps back

00:18:34.780 --> 00:18:37.380
to that catastrophic 2019 base year again.

00:18:37.380 --> 00:18:38.740
They want the trap dismantled

00:18:38.740 --> 00:18:41.020
so CMS doesn't have to try and find loopholes

00:18:41.020 --> 00:18:42.460
just to keep the system running.

00:18:42.460 --> 00:18:44.420
But they didn't just point fingers at Washington, right?

00:18:44.420 --> 00:18:45.260
Yeah.

00:18:45.260 --> 00:18:47.100
And this is where the public accountability aspect

00:18:47.100 --> 00:18:49.100
of their mission really shines.

00:18:49.100 --> 00:18:51.900
They had severe demands for the local government too.

00:18:51.900 --> 00:18:54.100
They were essentially telling San Juan,

00:18:54.100 --> 00:18:56.340
you know this cliff is in the statute,

00:18:56.340 --> 00:18:58.860
you cannot feign surprise when it happens.

00:18:58.860 --> 00:18:59.900
Oh, absolutely.

00:18:59.900 --> 00:19:03.140
Their demands for local transparency are rigorous.

00:19:03.140 --> 00:19:05.140
They focus heavily on ACES,

00:19:05.140 --> 00:19:07.580
the Administración de Seguros de Salud,

00:19:07.580 --> 00:19:08.780
which is the local agency

00:19:08.780 --> 00:19:11.220
that actually administers the Medicaid program

00:19:11.220 --> 00:19:13.180
known locally as Plan Vitao.

00:19:13.180 --> 00:19:14.140
Okay.

00:19:14.140 --> 00:19:16.940
Espacio Sabiertos demands that the Puerto Rican government,

00:19:16.940 --> 00:19:18.740
ACES and the health department,

00:19:18.740 --> 00:19:22.340
publish a concrete, highly detailed contingency plan

00:19:22.340 --> 00:19:25.940
before the fiscal year 2028 budget cycle even begins.

00:19:25.940 --> 00:19:28.180
So no improvising at the 11th hour

00:19:28.180 --> 00:19:29.180
when the funds dry up?

00:19:29.180 --> 00:19:30.340
No improvising.

00:19:30.340 --> 00:19:32.580
They want to know exactly what benefits will be cut,

00:19:32.580 --> 00:19:35.140
which eligibility rules will be rolled back.

00:19:35.140 --> 00:19:37.060
They also demand an exact accounting

00:19:37.060 --> 00:19:40.180
of the financial effects on Puerto Rico's 78 municipalities.

00:19:40.180 --> 00:19:42.100
Oh, right, because the local towns

00:19:42.100 --> 00:19:43.860
contribute to funding the system too.

00:19:43.860 --> 00:19:44.700
Yes.

00:19:44.700 --> 00:19:46.620
And if the federal money vanishes,

00:19:46.620 --> 00:19:49.620
the municipalities will bear a massive brunt of the fallout.

00:19:49.620 --> 00:19:52.340
Basically, EA is telling the local government,

00:19:52.340 --> 00:19:55.380
don't just stand on the beach watching the tsunami roll in,

00:19:55.380 --> 00:19:57.020
build an evacuation plan now

00:19:57.020 --> 00:19:59.140
and show the public exactly what it costs

00:19:59.140 --> 00:20:00.300
to build a seawall.

00:20:00.300 --> 00:20:01.340
That's spot on.

00:20:01.340 --> 00:20:04.780
And it goes further into institutional transparency too.

00:20:04.780 --> 00:20:06.420
Espacio Sabiertos is insisting

00:20:06.420 --> 00:20:09.540
on centralized accessible financial reporting.

00:20:09.540 --> 00:20:11.460
Currently, the financial oversight

00:20:11.460 --> 00:20:12.980
and management board for Puerto Rico

00:20:12.980 --> 00:20:16.300
requires internal financial reports from ACES.

00:20:16.300 --> 00:20:18.020
Espacio Sabiertos demands

00:20:18.020 --> 00:20:19.860
that those reports be made completely public.

00:20:19.860 --> 00:20:21.740
They want the raw data out in the open.

00:20:21.740 --> 00:20:24.780
Finally, they have a demand regarding pricing parity.

00:20:24.780 --> 00:20:26.660
Espacio Sabiertos insists

00:20:26.660 --> 00:20:28.540
that the local government officially calculate

00:20:28.540 --> 00:20:30.780
and publish the true total cost

00:20:30.780 --> 00:20:33.740
of providing full state-like mandatory services.

00:20:33.740 --> 00:20:35.140
So, stop guessing.

00:20:35.140 --> 00:20:37.100
Exactly. Stop relying on estimates.

00:20:37.100 --> 00:20:39.900
Price out the missing long-term nursing facility care.

00:20:39.900 --> 00:20:41.820
Price out what it would cost to align perfectly

00:20:41.820 --> 00:20:43.660
with the full federal poverty line.

00:20:43.660 --> 00:20:45.140
Put the real numbers on paper

00:20:45.140 --> 00:20:47.660
so that officials can take a factual undeniable bill

00:20:47.660 --> 00:20:49.740
to Congress when demanding parity.

00:20:49.740 --> 00:20:51.660
So, synthesizing all of this,

00:20:51.660 --> 00:20:55.060
Espacio Sabiertos utilized the critical heated window

00:20:55.060 --> 00:20:56.660
of September, 2026

00:20:56.660 --> 00:20:58.420
to cut through all the political noise

00:20:58.420 --> 00:21:00.340
and establish the actual math.

00:21:00.340 --> 00:21:03.620
They modeled the $4.849 billion

00:21:03.620 --> 00:21:05.900
local cost of the Medicaid cliff.

00:21:05.900 --> 00:21:07.220
They proved mathematically

00:21:07.220 --> 00:21:09.060
that the Section 1108 cap,

00:21:09.060 --> 00:21:10.260
not just the matching rate,

00:21:10.260 --> 00:21:12.860
is the true engine of this crisis.

00:21:12.860 --> 00:21:15.380
And they highlighted how decades of rationing care

00:21:15.380 --> 00:21:17.540
artificially suppresses the system.

00:21:17.540 --> 00:21:19.780
And finally, they laid out firm,

00:21:19.780 --> 00:21:22.540
unavoidable demands for congressional action

00:21:22.540 --> 00:21:24.660
and local government transparency.

00:21:24.660 --> 00:21:26.500
If we connect this to the bigger picture,

00:21:26.500 --> 00:21:28.860
it all cycles back to the title of their report,

00:21:28.860 --> 00:21:30.700
The Cost of Uncertainty.

00:21:30.700 --> 00:21:32.700
It isn't just a clever name for a white paper.

00:21:32.700 --> 00:21:35.780
It describes an active economic paralysis.

00:21:35.780 --> 00:21:38.380
Well, think about ACs trying to run plan vital.

00:21:38.380 --> 00:21:40.660
They contract with managed care organizations,

00:21:40.660 --> 00:21:42.500
insurers, to provide care.

00:21:42.500 --> 00:21:44.380
When ACs has absolutely no idea

00:21:44.380 --> 00:21:47.380
if its federal budget will drop by 90% in 13 months,

00:21:47.380 --> 00:21:49.060
they cannot sign long-term contracts.

00:21:49.060 --> 00:21:51.020
Right, you can't negotiate a multi-year deal

00:21:51.020 --> 00:21:53.740
if your funding might just evaporate next October.

00:21:53.740 --> 00:21:56.300
And it ripples down to the providers too.

00:21:56.300 --> 00:21:59.060
The system cannot attract or retain specialized doctors

00:21:59.060 --> 00:22:01.700
who are terrified they won't be reimbursed.

00:22:01.700 --> 00:22:04.380
Hospitals cannot plan to build new facilities,

00:22:04.380 --> 00:22:07.460
update their technology, or expand their wards.

00:22:07.460 --> 00:22:10.340
The uncertainty itself degrades the infrastructure

00:22:10.340 --> 00:22:11.980
before the cliff even arrives.

00:22:11.980 --> 00:22:13.380
So the threat of the cliff

00:22:13.380 --> 00:22:15.500
does as much damage to long-term planning

00:22:15.500 --> 00:22:17.020
as the cliff itself.

00:22:17.020 --> 00:22:19.820
Which leaves us with a fundamental question to consider.

00:22:19.820 --> 00:22:22.180
We spent an incredible amount of time in these reports

00:22:22.180 --> 00:22:24.300
arguing about FMAP formulas,

00:22:24.300 --> 00:22:27.580
allotment caps, base years, and matching rates.

00:22:27.580 --> 00:22:29.380
But ultimately, can a healthcare system

00:22:29.380 --> 00:22:31.780
ever truly make a population healthy

00:22:31.780 --> 00:22:34.820
if the system itself is chronically surviving month to month?

00:22:34.820 --> 00:22:35.900
Wow, yeah.

00:22:35.900 --> 00:22:38.820
A system fighting entirely for its own financial survival

00:22:38.820 --> 00:22:41.180
cannot focus on the physical survival of its patients.

00:22:41.180 --> 00:22:44.140
That is a heavy and necessary thought to leave on.

